<BaseLayout title="Affiliate Disclosure | Aging at Home Lab" description="How Aging at Home Lab handles affiliate links, sponsorships, and product recommendations.">
  <article class="prose">
    <p class="eyebrow">Affiliate Disclosure</p>
    <h1>How we handle recommendations and revenue.</h1>
    <p>Aging at Home Lab may eventually earn revenue through affiliate links, sponsorships, templates, products, or referral relationships. When a page includes a material relationship, it should be disclosed clearly and close to the relevant recommendation.</p>

    <h2>Our rule</h2>
    <p>Revenue should never be the reason a guide exists. A guide should help readers make a clearer, safer, more organized decision whether or not they buy anything.</p>

    <h2>Affiliate links</h2>
    <p>If we use affiliate links, we may earn a commission when a reader buys through those links. That should not change the price a reader pays unless stated otherwise.</p>

    <h2>Product recommendations</h2>
    <p>Product recommendations should be based on transparent criteria such as safety considerations, fit, instructions, limitations, usability, support, cost, and reader need. Product claims should be checked against manufacturer documentation or credible sources when relevant.</p>

    <h2>Sponsored content</h2>
    <p>If we publish sponsored content or accept compensation for coverage, it should be labeled clearly. Sponsored relationships should not be hidden inside normal editorial guidance.</p>

    <h2>Trust boundary</h2>
    <p>We avoid fear-based selling, exaggerated safety promises, and recommendations that imply a product can eliminate risk. Families should talk with qualified professionals for decisions involving health, mobility, home modifications, legal authority, or personal safety.</p>
  </article>
</BaseLayout>
